1 NOTE <p>James Sweeney began his career going door-to-door reading meters for Equitable Gas, and later went to night school and became one of the company's first computer programmers.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p
2 CONC >Before he retired, he had an 11th-floor office with a breathtaking view of Downtown.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&ldquo;He just worked his way up through the company,&rdquo; said David Sweeney of Naples, 
2 CONC Fla., the second eldest of Mr. Sweeney's three sons.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>James L. Sweeney of Morningside died Thursday, July 30, 2015, of natural causes. He was 77.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Mr. Sween
2 CONC ey was part of a team of programmers that computerized Equitable Gas's customer accounts in the mid-1960s. His interest was in math, and programming came naturally to him.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&ldqu
2 CONC o;That was his aptitude. That was his gift,&rdquo; said Dan Sweeney of Highland Park, Mr. Sweeney's eldest son. &ldquo;For the first practical use of computers, he was right there.&rdquo;</p>
<p>&nbs
2 CONC p;</p>
<p>He spent 37 years with the company, which later became Peoples Natural Gas. He was a supervisor in the information technology department when he retired in 1995.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Mr. 
2 CONC Sweeney was born Nov. 17, 1937, in Pittsburgh to Patrick and Mary Sweeney. He graduated in 1955 from Central Catholic High School in Oakland, then joined the Army.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>He met his wi
2 CONC fe, Mary Sweeney, at a picnic when he was discharged. They married May 12, 1962, in Immaculate Conception Catholic Church in Bloomfield.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Mr. Sweeney enjoyed playing poker, black
2 CONC jack, hearts and pinochle, and loved crossword puzzles.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>He was parish council president at St. Raphael Catholic Church in Morningside and helped to oversee the church's finances
2 CONC . He received the Manifesting the Kingdom Award for service to the church.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>In addition to his wife and two eldest sons, he is survived by his daughter, Diana Backner of Mt. Leba
2 CONC non; son Douglas Sweeney of Morningside; a sister, Marjorie Webber of the North Hills; a brother, John Sweeney of the North Hills; and 12 grandchildren.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Visitation is from 1 to 
2 CONC 4 and 6 to 8 p.m. Monday in McCabe Brothers Funeral Home, 6214 Walnut St., Shadyside. A Mass of Christian Burial will be at 10 a.m. Tuesday in St. Raphael Church, 1118 Chislett St., Morningside.</p>
