Dear Dana,
4 CONC 
I&#039;m almost embarrassed to reply to your email.  I know very little about  my father&#039;s mother, Margaret Jane McCusker&#039;s family.
Margaret was born about 1876 in Flushing, Queens County, 
4 CONC New York, USA.
 Her parents John McCusker and Jane Curran, it&#039;s been told to me, came from Enniskillen, Fermanagh, Northern Ireland.  I don&#039;t know when they came to the US, but, the story go
4 CONC es that some of the family emigrated to Australia too.

John ran a saloon in Flushing.
The family was Roman Catholic. They had three daughters - Margaret, Agnes (Aggie) b.in 1880s, 
Florence (Fonny) a
4 CONC bout two years younger than Agnes and 
Catherine (Cassie) b about 1892.
Their ages were not accurately recorded in the 1910, 1920, 1930 censuses.

From our family grave records - Mount St. Mary&#039;s
4 CONC  RC Cemetery, Flushing, NY, the following persons were buried:
James MCusker - relationship unknown b. about 1829, d. 1887 at age 58yrs
John McCusker - whom I believe is my g grandfather - b.about 184
4 CONC 7, d. 1897 at age 50
Mrs. Jane McCusker - whom I believe is my g grandmother (nee Jane  Curran) - b. about 1849, d. 30 July 1898
Mrs. McCusker - given name Ann (from newspaper death notice) - who&#039
4 CONC ;d be my &gt; gg grandmother - died 28 Sep 1898
 John McCusker- no age or dates listed - possibly my grandmother&#039;s younger brother (from a family picture)
Unfortunately, my grandmother died at ag
4 CONC e 53 in 1930, before I was born. 

There is no family left to gain first hand information from.  To my  knowledge, they had not kept in touch with their Australian relatives. As you can see, there&#03
4 CONC 9;s a lot of information to be found, but I&#039;d love to learn about my Irish ancestors. I see that your Thomas and my gg grandfather would have been about the same age.
