By DONALD MILLER
Post-Gazette Art Critic
THE ANONYMOUS jurors for the 68th
annual exhibition of th
4 CONC e Associated Artists of
Pittsburgh, opening tomorrow in the Heinz
Galleries of the Museum of Art, have done yeoman's
service.
The show they have chosen is not only attractive
but can be easily underst
4 CONC ood along the
lines of relative quality and originality. There
is no question that this show is a better one
than last year's. It is strongly reminiscent of
the 1976 exhibition, the Associated's first
4 CONC  in the
renovated galleries.
You know of the academic salons that the
impressionists failed to impress. Well, the Associated
is Pittsburgh's salon-where the
established artists and those hoping to mak
4 CONC e a
place for themselves meet for equal judgment.
Although this exhibition is solid it is also
very middle of the road. There is very little
that is going to quicken the pulses of those who
are steady
4 CONC  observers of the local scene. Perhaps
it is enough that the show looks handsome
overall in this winter of severest discontent,
but no critic is going to be satisfied with that.
THIS IS ONE year when 
4 CONC this writer liked
the show better in bulk rather than in specific
works. There are no brilliant surprises. And
one of our very best painters, Harry Holland,
is missing, for whatever reason, But mainly
4 CONC 
the show is so very like so many recent annuals,
except for last year's which didn't make
sense to anyone.
Perhaps the Associated board thinks it can
go on forever showing much the same show
that it 
4 CONC has been offering for nine years, since
its epic " Our Environment. " Perhaps it can.
But it isn't going to excite a lot of people who
have come to expect more of the Associated
because there is a gre
