by Krienen-Griffith Funeral Home-New Castle. &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Ve
4 CONC ronica&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;
(Shields) &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Diggins&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;, 91, died Wednesday, August 16 of complications from a 2022 stroke at the
Newark home of h
4 CONC er daughter and son-in-law where she had lived for the past 19 years.&amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;’s husband
of 68 years, John N. &amp;lt;span class="highlight
4 CONC -text"&amp;gt;Diggins&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;, died in 2018. They had lived in Wilmington 52 years before moving to
Newark.&amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; attended St
4 CONC . Paul’s Parochial School in Wilmington and graduated from Ursuline Academy
in 1948. She attended St. &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;’s College.&amp;lt;span cl
4 CONC ass="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; was the daughter
of the late William A. and Ethel Tocik Shields and the 13th sibling of 17 brothers and sisters.&amp;lt;span class="highlight-text
4 CONC "&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;
taught kindergarten and the elementary grades at Holy Spirit Parochial School in New Castle, at the parish founded and built by her late
brother, the Rev. Charles F.
4 CONC  Shields. At the urging of her brother, Father Shields, &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; started her
working career as a teenage counter helper at a local candy sto
4 CONC re around the corner from where she grew up on Jackson Street in Wilmington.
She went on to work as a telephone operator for what was then Bell Telephone Company.But she devoted most of her time, love
4 CONC , and passion to
being a wife, mother, grandmother and great-grandmother, and caretaker for many of her siblings’ children. In her spare time and
especially after the children grew or moved away, sh
4 CONC e turned her attention to Christ Our King Church, the parish where she worshipped and
where she sent her children to school.&amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; ran the
4 CONC  church’s food closet, soliciting donations of
food and doling it out to those in need. &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; helped run the sandwich brigade, voluntee
4 CONC rs that would
make sandwiches for Emmanuel Dining Room in Wilmington. She and her husband would collect the sandwiches from parish volunteers and deliver
them.&amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;g
4 CONC t;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; also helped clean the church and joined a cadre of ladies who cleaned and polished preparing
for church services and she conscripted John to help other husbands maintain th
4 CONC e building. The Diocese of Wilmington recognized their work
with the Order of Merit for their “outstanding contributions” to the diocese. To her chagrin, the Diocese of Wilmington shut the parish

4 CONC and sold the church buildings after she and John moved.&amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; is survived by her daughter and son-in-law
with whom she lived, &amp;lt;span
4 CONC  class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Mary&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; V. (Ronnie) and Gerald (Jerry) Hager; her son and his wife,
Charlie and Annie &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Diggins&amp;lt;/span&a
4 CONC mp;gt;; her grandchildren, Joel, Beth and her husband Keith Rolph, Kara and her
husband Joe Leotta, Jonathan &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Diggins&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; and Megan &amp;lt;s
4 CONC pan class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Diggins&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;;
great-grandchildren, Matt Rolph, Katie Rolph, Liz Rolph, Emma &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Diggins&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; an
4 CONC d Piper &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Diggins&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;.She also is survived by three siblings, Cecilia Creedon, Albert Shields, and Patricia Smith.The
family would like to tha
4 CONC nk the staff of Delaware Hospice, especially Decia Kinslow, R.N. and Jameson (Jamie) Miller, C.N.A. for their
