4 CONC ass="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;, a native of
Bristol, PA and a longtime resident of Mobile, died February 6, 2012 in a local health care facility. He is survived by his wife of 
4 CONC 47
years, Roberta Fink &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;, his daughter Barbara Catherine "Katie" &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;g
4 CONC t;, her husband David Earle and their daughter Charlotte of Wellington, New Zealand, and his daughter
Bridget &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; Lafferty, her husban
4 CONC d Pat and their sons Aidan and Conor of Milwaukee WI. He is also
survived by a number of nieces and nephews. Charlie was predeceased by his parents, Connell and Katie &amp;lt;span class="highlight-tex
4 CONC t"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;, and by his four siblings. Dr. &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; was born November 25, 1924.
He graduated from Spring Hill Coll
4 CONC ege in 1950, and received a MA from Marquette University and a Ph.D from the University of Wisconsin
before returning to Spring Hill in 1957 as a member of the faculty in the Department of English. He
4 CONC  served the College in various academic
and administrative capacities, including Chairman of the Division of Humanities and English, Dean of Faculties, and Academic Dean. He was
named Teacher of the Y
4 CONC ear in 1963 and 1986, chaired the College's sesquicentennial celebration in 1980, directed the Spring Hill Venice
Program in 1987-89, and was named Professor Emeritus in 1990. In addition, he served a
4 CONC s Visiting Scholar at the University of Virginia in
1977-78. After his retirement from full-time faculty status, Charlie was appointed as the College Archivist in 1991 and continued in this
capacity u
4 CONC ntil 2005. He has been honored by the establishment of the &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; Lecture Series which is
held annually in his honor. During his tenure a
4 CONC t Spring Hill, Charlie compiled and edited three publications on this history of the College.
Twice Remembered, Moments in the History of Spring Hill College, a compilation of events of the first hund
4 CONC red years of the college's
existence, was published in 1993. Sound Mind, Sound Body, a History of Sports at Spring Hill College, co-edited by Patrick McGraw, was
published in 1997, and Gleanings from 
4 CONC the Spring Hill College Archives was published in 2004. Charlie was a member of a number of academic
and literary associations and served a two year term as President of the Association of College Eng
4 CONC lish Teachers in Alabama. His primary
focus in teaching was in the field of American Literature; he taught courses in American fiction, poetry and drama, he developed a seminar
course in modern Americ
4 CONC an fiction with emphasis on William Faulkner and Flannery O'Connor, and he designed a course of study of the dominant
themes of American literature. There will be a Mass of Christian Burial at St. Jos
4 CONC eph's Chapel at Spring Hill College on Wednesday, February
15 at 4:30 p.m. with Rev, Michael Williams, S.J. presiding and members of the Jesuit Community concelebrating. The family will receive
friend
4 CONC s beginning at 4:00 in the Chapel, and there will be a reception immediately following the Mass in the Gautrelet Room in the Lucey
Administration Building. In lieu of flowers, the family requests dona
4 CONC tions to the &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Charles&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; J. &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; Scholarship Fund at Spring Hill College.
