 OBITUARY for Charles John &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;; recen
4 CONC t residence: AL. &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;BOYLE&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;
Charles John &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;, a native of Bristol, PA and a
4 CONC  longtime resident of Mobile, died February 6, 2012
in a local health care facility. He is survived by his wife of 47 years, Roberta Fink &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&
4 CONC amp;gt;, his
daughter &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Barbara&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; Catherine "Katie" &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;, her husband David
4 CONC 
Earle and their daughter Charlotte of Wellington, New Zealand, and his daughter Bridget &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt;
Lafferty, her husband Pat and their sons 
4 CONC Aidan and Conor of Milwaukee WI. He is also survived by a number of nieces and nephews. Charlie was
predeceased by his parents, Connell and Katie &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;l
4 CONC t;/span&amp;gt;, and by his four siblings. Dr. &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; was born November 25, 1924. He graduated from Spring Hill College in 1950, and rece
4 CONC ived a MA from
Marquette University and a Ph.D from the University of Wisconsin before returning to Spring Hill in 1957 as a member of the faculty in the
Department of English. He served the College i
4 CONC n various academic and administrative capacities, including Chairman of the Division of
Humanities and English, Dean of Faculties, and Academic Dean. He was named Teacher of the Year in 1963 and 1986,
4 CONC  chaired the College's
sesquicentennial celebration in 1980, directed the Spring Hill Venice Program in 1987-89, and was named Professor Emeritus in 1990. In
addition, he served as Visiting Scholar at
4 CONC  the University of Virginia in 1977-78. After his retirement from full-time faculty status,
Charlie was appointed as the College Archivist in 1991 and continued in this capacity until 2005. He has bee
4 CONC n honored by the establishment
of the &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; Lecture Series which is held annually in his honor. During his tenure at Spring Hill,
Charli
4 CONC e compiled and edited three publications on this history of the College. Twice Remembered, Moments in the History of Spring Hill
College, a compilation of events of the first hundred years of the coll
4 CONC ege's existence, was published in 1993. Sound Mind, Sound Body, a
History of Sports at Spring Hill College, co-edited by Patrick McGraw, was published in 1997, and Gleanings from the Spring Hill Colle
4 CONC ge
Archives was published in 2004. Charlie was a member of a number of academic and literary associations and served a two year term as
President of the Association of College English Teachers in Alab
4 CONC ama. His primary focus in teaching was in the field of American Literature;
he taught courses in American fiction, poetry and drama, he developed a seminar course in modern American fiction with empha
4 CONC sis on William
Faulkner and Flannery O'Connor, and he designed a course of study of the dominant themes of American literature. There will be a Mass of
Christian Burial at St. Joseph's Chapel at Sprin
4 CONC g Hill College on Wednesday, February 15 at 4:30 p.m. with Rev, Michael Williams, S.J.
presiding and members of the Jesuit Community concelebrating. The family will receive friends beginning at 4:00 i
4 CONC n the Chapel, and there
will be a reception immediately following the Mass in the Gautrelet Room in the Lucey Administration Building. In lieu of flowers, the
family requests donations to the Charles 
4 CONC J. &amp;lt;span class="highlight-text"&amp;gt;Boyle&amp;lt;/span&amp;gt; Scholarship Fund at Spring Hill College.
