1 TITL England & Wales, Death Index, 1837-2005
1 PUBL MyHeritage
1 TEXT Civil registration — the government recording of births, marriages, and deaths — began in England and Wales on 1 July 1837. Local registration districts had jurisdiction for recording civil events
2 CONC , but were required to send copies of their records each quarter to the General Register Office (GRO) in London. The GRO created indexes to these records which are organized by event, year, and quarte
2 CONC r, and thereunder alphabetically by surname.
2 CONC Information included in the index changed over the years. The index always provides the name of deceased, registration district, and reference information (volume and page numbers). From 1866 to March
2 CONC  1969, the index also includes age. Beginning in June 1969 the index includes the deceased’s birth date rather than age. 
2 CONC Information provided in the index can be used to order a copy of the person’s death certificate for a fee from the GRO through their Certificate Ordering Service. Depending on the year, full death c
2 CONC ertificates may provide: name of deceased, death date, death place, age, sex, occupation, cause of death, name of parent if the deceased is a child, informant’s name, residence, and relationship to 
2 CONC the deceased, and date of registration. 
2 CONC  
2 CONC Note: Information recorded on a death certificate is only as accurate as the knowledge of the person reporting it. It is important to pay attention to who the informant was and their relationship to t
2 CONC he deceased. The closer the relationship, the more reliable the information likely is. 
2 CONC For years where images of the index are available, be sure to consult the image to verify the information presented to you. Sometimes errors happen during the transcription process. For example, a “
2 CONC 5” may have inadvertently been transcribed as a “3”. Since there is a fee for ordering certificate copies from the GRO, it is especially important to make sure all reference numbers are correct 
2 CONC before placing an order. 
2 CONC Search tip: If an individual had multiple given names, sometimes only one or two of these names was recorded in the index. In addition, some of the given names may have been recorded by initials only.
2 CONC  If you’re having trouble locating someone in the index, try searching by any of the individual’s known given names, initials, or nicknames.
1 _TYPE Collection
1 _MEDI 10444
0 @S500107@ SOUR
1 _UPD 15 OCT 2018 10:44:05 GMT -0500 
1 TITL England & Wales, Birth Index, 1837-2005
1 PUBL MyHeritage
1 TEXT Civil registration — the government recording of births, marriages, and deaths — began in England and Wales on 1 July 1837. Local registration districts had jurisdiction for recording civil events
2 CONC , but were required to send copies of their records each quarter to the General Register Office (GRO) in London. The GRO created indexes to these records which are organized by event, year, and quarte
2 CONC r, and thereunder alphabetically by surname.
2 CONC Information included in the index changed over the years. From inception up to June 1911, the birth index lists: name of child, registration district, and reference information (volume and page number
2 CONC s). Since September 1911 the index also includes the mother’s maiden surname.
2 CONC Information provided in the index can be used to order a copy of the person’s birth certificate for a fee from the GRO through their Certificate Ordering Service. Depending on the year, full birth c
2 CONC ertificates may provide: name of child, birth date, birthplace, sex, father’s name and occupation, mother’s maiden name, informant’s name, residence, and relationship to the child, and date of r
2 CONC egistration.
2 CONC Note: For years where images of the index are available, be sure to consult the image to verify the information presented to you. Sometimes errors happen during the transcription process. For example,
2 CONC  a "5" may have inadvertently been transcribed as a "3". Since there is a fee for ordering certificate copies from the GRO, it is especially important to make sure all reference numbers are correct be
2 CONC fore placing an order.
2 CONC Search tip: Compliance with registration laws have always been very good. It is estimated that initially about 90-95% of births were reported, but that by 1875 registration was 99%. Since nearly all b
2 CONC irths were reported, if you are having trouble finding someone in the index, try searching using various given names or initials. In later years, searching on the combination of child’s surname and 
2 CONC mother’s maiden name (if known), may also help locate difficult-to-find individuals. If an individual can still not be found, especially in earlier years, try searching local parish records for the 
2 CONC child’s baptismal record.
1 _TYPE Collection
1 _MEDI 10442
