1 TITL United States, World War I Draft Registrations, 1917-1918
1 PUBL MyHeritage
1 TEXT The initial Selective Service Act required all men aged 21 to 30 to register. In August 1918, Congress amended the law to expand the age range to include all men aged 18 to 45. Three specific registra
2 CONC tions were conducted: June 5, 1917. All men between the ages of 21 and 31. June 5, 1918. Those who had turned 21 after June 5, 1917, and a supplemental registration held on August 24, 1918, for those 
2 CONC who turned 21 years old after June 5, 1918. September 12, 1918. All men aged 18 through 45 not previously enrolled.
2 CONC By the end of the First World War, 2 million men had volunteered for military service and 2.8 million other men had been drafted. A draft registration does not imply that the individual ended up being
2 CONC  drafted or that he didn’t volunteer separately. The handwriting on the card is normally that of a registration board worker or “registrar”. However, almost all cards contain the signature or �
2 CONC �mark” in the handwriting of the registrant himself.
1 _TYPE Collection
